(Regression test: embedding a buffer variable directly inside a format
 string, e.g. "{buf}", must print its actual byte content. The codegen
 path for {var} inside a string literal loaded the buffer's raw struct
 pointer (capacity/length/flags header) into rdi without adjusting to
 the data area (+24, same offset _buffer_data applies) before printing.
 Whenever capacity's low byte was 0 - true for any capacity that is a
 multiple of 256, e.g. the 1024/4096 defaults - PRINT_CSTR hit that
 zero byte immediately and printed nothing, even though the buffer's
 own size/length fields and equality checks were completely correct.)

a buffer called "greeting" is "hello".
Print "embedded: {greeting}".

a buffer called "roomy" is 4096 bytes in size.
copy "sized buffer content" to roomy.
Print "embedded sized buffer: {roomy}".

(Multiple buffers embedded in the same format string)
Print "both: {greeting} / {roomy}".
